doc:agreementEsoc a echr:AdministrativeAction ;
    rdfs:label "Agreement concerning ESOC"^^xsd:string ;
    echr:hasParticipation doc:agreementParticipationEsro,
        doc:agreementParticipationGermany ;
    echr:hasSupportingQuote """Part III of the Agreement contains general provisions. Article 6 provides as follows:
“1. Subject to the provisions of the Protocol on Privileges and Immunities of the Organisation and of any complementary Agreement between the Federal Republic of Germany and the Organisation according to Article 30 of that Protocol, the activities of the Organisation in the Federal Republic of Germany shall be governed by German law. If the terms of employment of a staff member of the Organisation are not governed by the Organisation’s staff regulations, then they shall be subject to German laws and regulations.
2. Disputes between the Organisation and such staff members of the Organisation in the Federal Republic of Germany who are not within the competence of the Organisation’s Appeals Board, shall be subject to German jurisdiction.”"""^^xsd:string,
        "The Agreement was concluded between the Government of the Federal Republic of Germany and ESRO for the purpose of establishing a European Space Operations Centre, including the European Space Data Centre. Articles 1 to 4 of the Agreement concern the site for construction of the ESOC buildings and related matters."^^xsd:string .

doc:caseBeerAndReganVGermany a echr:CaseDocument ;
    rdfs:label "CASE OF BEER AND REGAN v. GERMANY (Application no. 28934/95)"^^xsd:string ;
    echr:hasApplicant doc:applicantKarlheinzBeer,
        doc:applicantPhilipRegan ;
    echr:hasApplicationNumber "28934/95"^^xsd:string ;
    echr:hasJudgmentDate "1999-02-18"^^xsd:date ;
    echr:hasRespondentState "Germany"^^xsd:string ;
    echr:hasSupportingQuote "According to the Government, the applicants were also left with other possibilities, such as claiming compensation from the foreign firm which had hired them out."^^xsd:string,
        "Accordingly, there has been no violation of that provision."^^xsd:string,
        "Admittedly, the applicants were able to argue the question of immunity."^^xsd:string,
        "As to the notion of “staff member”, it would have been for the ESA Appeals Board, under Regulation 33 § 6 of the ESA Staff Regulations, to settle the question of its jurisdiction and, in this connection, to rule whether in substance the applicants fell within the notion of “staff members”."^^xsd:string,
        "CASE OF BEER AND REGAN v. GERMANY (Application no. 28934/95)"^^xsd:string,
        "In accordance with Regulation 33 § 1 of the ESA Staff Regulations, the ESA Appeals Board, which is “independent of the Agency”, has jurisdiction “to hear disputes relating to any explicit or implicit decision taken by the Agency and arising between it and a staff member\"."^^xsd:string,
        "In such court proceedings, a judicial clarification of the nature of the labour relationship can be obtained."^^xsd:string,
        "In view of all these circumstances, the Court finds that, in giving effect to the immunity from jurisdiction of ESA on the basis of section 20(2) of the Courts Act, the German courts did not exceed their margin of appreciation."^^xsd:string,
        "Moreover, it is in principle open to temporary workers to seek redress from the firms that have employed them and hired them out."^^xsd:string,
        "Relying on general labour regulations or, more particularly, on the German Provision of Labour (Temporary Staff) Act, temporary workers can file claims in damages against such firms."^^xsd:string,
        "Taking into account in particular the alternative means of legal process available to the applicants, it cannot be said that the limitation on their access to the German courts with regard to ESA impaired the essence of their “right to a court” or was disproportionate for the purposes of Article 6 § 1 of the Convention."^^xsd:string,
        "The applicants maintained that the right of access to the courts was not met merely by the institution of proceedings."^^xsd:string,
        "The remedies available to the applicants were in particular an appeal to the ESA Appeals Board if they wished to assert contractual rights, their years of membership of the ESA staff and their integration into the operation of ESA."^^xsd:string,
        "The significant feature of the instant case is that the applicants, after having performed services at the premises of ESOC in Darmstadt for a considerable time on the basis of contracts with foreign firms, attempted to obtain recognition of permanent employment by ESA on the basis of the above-mentioned special German legislation for the regulation of the German labour market."^^xsd:string,
        "They concluded that the proper functioning of ESA had not required immunity from German jurisdiction in their particular cases."^^xsd:string,
        "They considered that the German courts had disregarded the priority of human rights over immunity rules based on international agreements."^^xsd:string,
        "This right, they argued, required that the courts examine the merits of their claim."^^xsd:string .

doc:proceeding_beer_vs_esa_1993 a echr:DomesticProceeding ;
    rdfs:label "Darmstadt Labour Court proceeding by Mr Karlheinz Beer against ESA (instituted Oct/Nov 1993)"^^xsd:string,
        "Darmstadt Labour Court proceeding by Mr Philip Regan against ESA (instituted Oct/Nov 1993)"^^xsd:string ;
    echr:hasCourt doc:darmstadtLabourCourt ;
    echr:hasDecisionDate "1995-03-21"^^xsd:date ;
    echr:hasOutcome echr:OutcomeDeclaredInadmissible ;
    echr:hasOutcomeDirection echr:DirectionUnfavourableToApplicant ;
    echr:hasParticipation doc:participation_beer_darmstadt_1995,
        doc:participation_esa_beer_darmstadt_1995,
        doc:participation_regan_darmstadt_1995 ;
    echr:hasSupportingQuote "Admittedly, the applicants were able to argue the question of immunity."^^xsd:string,
        "In October and November 1993 the applicants instituted proceedings before the Darmstadt Labour Court ( Arbeitsgericht ) against ESA, arguing that, pursuant to the German Provision of Labour (Temporary Staff) Act ( Arbeitnehmerüberlassungsgesetz ), they had acquired the status of employees of ESA."^^xsd:string,
        "In both decisions, the Labour Court considered that ESA had validly relied on its immunity from jurisdiction."^^xsd:string,
        "In such court proceedings, a judicial clarification of the nature of the labour relationship can be obtained."^^xsd:string,
        "In view of all these circumstances, the Court finds that, in giving effect to the immunity from jurisdiction of ESA on the basis of section 20(2) of the Courts Act, the German courts did not exceed their margin of appreciation."^^xsd:string,
        "Moreover, it is in principle open to temporary workers to seek redress from the firms that have employed them and hired them out."^^xsd:string,
        "On 21 March 1995 the Darmstadt Labour Court, following hearings, declared the applicants’ respective actions inadmissible."^^xsd:string,
        "Relying on general labour regulations or, more particularly, on the German Provision of Labour (Temporary Staff) Act, temporary workers can file claims in damages against such firms."^^xsd:string,
        "The German labour court proceedings in the present case, as in the case of Mr Waite and Mr Kennedy, had concentrated on the question of whether or not ESA could validly rely on its immunity from jurisdiction."^^xsd:string,
        "The German labour court regarded the applicants’ action under the German Provision of Labour (Temporary Staff) Act inadmissible as the defendant, ESA, had claimed immunity from jurisdiction in accordance with Article XV § 2 of the ESA Convention and Article IV § 1 of its Annex I."^^xsd:string,
        "The Labour Court further recalled that the Federal Labour Court ( Bundesarbeitsgericht ), in a decision of 10 November 1993 in a similar case (file no. 7 AZR 600/92; see the Waite and Kennedy v. Germany judgment of 18 February 1999, to be published in the Court’s official reports, §§ 21-25), had found that the rules in question did not conflict with fundamental principles of German constitutional law."^^xsd:string,
        "The Labour Court referred to the decision of the Federal Labour Court of 10 November 1993 in the case concerning Mr Waite and Mr Kennedy (see paragraph 15 above)."^^xsd:string,
        "The Labour Court, referring to section 20(2) of the Courts Act ( Gerichtsverfassungsgesetz ), according to which immunity from jurisdiction could be provided for, inter alia , in international agreements, considered that ESA enjoyed such immunity under Article XV § 2 of the ESA Convention and its Annex I."^^xsd:string,
        "The applicants had access to the Darmstadt Labour Court, only to be told that their action was barred by operation of law."^^xsd:string,
        "The applicants maintained that the right of access to the courts was not met merely by the institution of proceedings."^^xsd:string,
        "The remedies available to the applicants were in particular an appeal to the ESA Appeals Board if they wished to assert contractual rights, their years of membership of the ESA staff and their integration into the operation of ESA."^^xsd:string,
        "The significant feature of the instant case is that the applicants, after having performed services at the premises of ESOC in Darmstadt for a considerable time on the basis of contracts with foreign firms, attempted to obtain recognition of permanent employment by ESA on the basis of the above-mentioned special German legislation for the regulation of the German labour market."^^xsd:string,
        "They concluded that the proper functioning of ESA had not required immunity from German jurisdiction in their particular cases."^^xsd:string,
        "They considered that the German courts had disregarded the priority of human rights over immunity rules based on international agreements."^^xsd:string,
        "This right, they argued, required that the courts examine the merits of their claim."^^xsd:string .

doc:proceeding_beer_vs_t_1995 a echr:DomesticProceeding ;
    rdfs:label "Labour court proceedings concerning the dismissal of Mr Karlheinz Beer (referenced in agreement of 20 June 1995)"^^xsd:string ;
    echr:hasParticipation doc:participation_beer_t_1995,
        doc:participation_t_beer_1995 ;
    echr:hasProceedingType echr:TypeCivil ;
    echr:hasSupportingQuote "**18.** Under the terms of an agreement concluded on 20 June 1995 with the first applicant, terminating his contract of employment on 30 June 1995, the company T. undertook to pay to the first applicant the sum of DEM 14,000 for lawyer’s fees incurred in labour court proceedings concerning his dismissal and DEM 305,000 for the loss of his job."^^xsd:string .

doc:proceeding_regan_vs_ti_1994 a echr:DomesticProceeding ;
    rdfs:label "Darmstadt Labour Court proceedings by Mr Philip Regan against T.I. re dismissal"^^xsd:string ;
    echr:hasCourt doc:darmstadtLabourCourt ;
    echr:hasDecisionDate "1994-09-06"^^xsd:date ;
    echr:hasOutcome echr:OutcomeSettled ;
    echr:hasParticipation doc:participation_regan_ti_1994,
        doc:participation_ti_regan_1994 ;
    echr:hasSupportingQuote "According to this settlement, the second applicant’s employment with the company T.I. had terminated on 31 December 1993, and the company T.I. undertook to pay to the second applicant the sum of 22,000 marks (DEM) for loss of job."^^xsd:string,
        "In the proceedings instituted by the second applicant against his dismissal by the company T.I. (see paragraph 13 above), a settlement had been reached in the Darmstadt Labour Court on 6 September 1994."^^xsd:string .

